\title{Cell-Free Massive MIMO via Ising: Joint Access-Point Selection and Precoding with a QUBO Outer Loop}

\author{Anonymous}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Cell-free massive MIMO promises uniformly high-throughput service through user-centric cooperation among many distributed access points (APs). A central implementation challenge is the joint design of discrete AP–user association and continuous multiuser precoding under per-AP power limits and fronthaul constraints. We propose a two-level optimization framework that explicitly decomposes these tasks: an outer Quadratic Unconstrained Binary Optimization (QUBO) layer selects the active AP–user links and an inner regularized zero-forcing (RZF) layer computes the precoder on the selected topology. The QUBO's diagonal terms capture standalone link utilities, the off-diagonals encode pairwise interference couplings derived from MRT-based projections, and quadratic penalties enforce AP capacity and per-user diversity (e.g., exactly $L$ serving APs). We show how the QUBO maps to an Ising energy $(J,h)$ compatible with coherent Ising machines (CIMs) or annealers, while retaining polynomial construction cost. To ensure feasibility and robust performance, we introduce a lightweight post-annealing projection that satisfies the combinatorial constraints without discarding the global structure discovered by the Ising search. Extensive simulations with realistic large-scale fading and per-AP power normalization demonstrate statistically significant gains over strong heuristics (max-channel-gain, greedy sum-rate, and greedy max-min fairness). We also present a fairness-oriented QUBO variant—using large-scale-fading-informed user weights—that improves Jain's fairness index with modest sum-rate trade-offs. Complexity analysis, ablations on penalty parameters and fairness weights, and a discussion of CSI/fronthaul requirements indicate the proposed framework's practicality and scalability for near-term cell-free deployments, including potential acceleration via photonic/electronic Ising hardware.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Cell-free massive MIMO represents a paradigm shift from traditional cellular architectures. By eliminating cell boundaries and allowing a large number of distributed access points (APs) to coherently and jointly serve all users in a region, it promises to deliver uniformly high data rates and superior coverage \cite{ngo2017cell,demir2021foundations}. This user-centric approach fundamentally mitigates the cell-edge problem, where users suffer from low signal quality and high inter-cell interference \cite{buzzi2019user}. However, realizing the full potential of cell-free systems requires solving a formidable resource allocation problem that spans both discrete and continuous domains: one must decide which APs should serve which users (a combinatorial selection problem) and simultaneously design the corresponding multiuser precoding vectors (a continuous optimization problem), all while respecting practical constraints like per-AP power limits and finite fronthaul capacity \cite{nayebi2017precoding,bjornson2019making}.

A naive "all-to-all" approach, where every AP serves every user, is often intractable due to prohibitive overheads in channel state information (CSI) acquisition, fronthaul signaling, and computational complexity. Consequently, a scalable solution must involve selecting a sparse, interference-aware subset of AP-user links for each time-frequency resource block \cite{interdonato2019scalability,chen2020structured}. While simple heuristics like assigning users to APs with the strongest large-scale channel gain are computationally cheap, they are often suboptimal as they neglect the intricate structure of multiuser interference \cite{zhang2019cell}. More sophisticated methods based on convex optimization or mixed-integer programming can achieve better performance but typically scale poorly with the number of APs and users. In recent years, machine learning approaches have shown promise but often require extensive training data and may not generalize well to dynamic network topologies \cite{elsherif2022resource,zappone2021uplink}.

This paper explores an alternative paradigm: physics-inspired optimization. Specifically, we leverage the mapping between many NP-hard combinatorial problems and the ground-state search of an Ising model \cite{lucas2014ising}. By formulating a problem as a Quadratic Unconstrained Binary Optimization (QUBO) problem, it can be solved by specialized hardware such as coherent Ising machines (CIMs), quantum annealers, or digital annealers, which are purpose-built to find low-energy states of Ising Hamiltonians \cite{mcmahon2016fully,inagaki2016coherent,kasi2021resource,grassl2022ising}. We propose a novel two-level architecture that uses this paradigm for the discrete AP selection task, combined with a classical linear precoder for the continuous part. This hybrid approach creates a flexible and powerful framework that can be optimized for different network utilities (e.g., sum-rate maximization or user fairness) and is amenable to hardware acceleration.

\subsection*{Contributions}
The main contributions of this work are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item We formulate the AP–user link selection problem as a QUBO. The formulation incorporates: (i) diagonal terms that encode the standalone utility of each potential link, (ii) off-diagonal terms that represent the pairwise interference cost based on Maximum-Ratio Transmission (MRT) projections, and (iii) quadratic penalties that enforce AP capacity and per-user service level constraints.
\item We integrate the QUBO selection stage with an inner regularized zero-forcing (RZF) precoding stage. Critically, the precoder is designed on the selected network topology, but performance is evaluated using the full physical channel matrix, ensuring an accurate and realistic assessment of signal and interference power.
\item We introduce a fairness-oriented variant of the QUBO by incorporating user-specific weights derived from large-scale fading conditions. This allows the framework to prioritize users in poor channel conditions, significantly improving the Jain's fairness index with only a modest trade-off in total sum rate.
\item We develop a lightweight feasibility-projection algorithm that is applied post-annealing. This deterministic step guarantees that all hard constraints are met while preserving the high-quality global structure of the solution found by the Ising solver.
\item We conduct a rigorous and reproducible empirical evaluation. Our study includes multiple strong baselines (random, max-channel-gain, greedy sum-rate, and greedy max-min fairness), averages results over 50 independent seeds, and reports performance with confidence intervals and statistical significance tests. We also provide detailed complexity analysis and ablation studies.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}
The concept of cell-free massive MIMO has evolved significantly since its inception, with a strong foundation in both theory and experimental validation \cite{ngo2017cell,demir2021foundations,bjornson2019massive}. Research has focused on practical aspects like scalable processing, power control, and user association. User-centric clustering, where each user is served by a dedicated subset of APs, has emerged as a key strategy to balance performance and overhead \cite{buzzi2019user,chen2020structured,bashar2019uplink}. The joint optimization of user association and precoding is a well-known NP-hard problem, and various approaches have been proposed. These include iterative algorithms based on convex approximations \cite{nayebi2017precoding}, centralized minimum mean squared error (MMSE) processing \cite{bjornson2019making}, and decentralized precoding schemes \cite{zhang2021improving}. Ensuring fairness among users is also a critical design goal, often addressed through max-min fairness or proportional fairness objectives \cite{bashar2021max}.

Parallel to these developments in wireless communications, physics-inspired optimization has gained significant traction for solving hard combinatorial problems across various fields. The ability to map problems to a QUBO, and subsequently to an Ising Hamiltonian, opens the door to specialized hardware accelerators \cite{lucas2014ising,glover2018tutorial,mcmahon2016fully,inagaki2016coherent}. In the context of wireless networks, this approach has been applied to problems such as link scheduling, resource allocation in IoT networks, and spectrum management \cite{kasi2021resource,grassl2022ising}. Recently, there has been initial work on applying quantum annealing to cell-free MIMO user association \cite{zhao2022joint}. Our work builds on this foundation but offers several distinct contributions: (i) a novel, first-principles QUBO formulation where pairwise interference terms are derived from physical-layer MRT projections, providing a more accurate proxy for multiuser interference than simple adjacency models; (ii) a flexible fairness-weighted QUBO variant; and (iii) a complete and rigorously evaluated two-level system architecture, including a feasibility projection step, comparison against multiple strong baselines, and detailed analysis of complexity and practical limitations.

\section{System Model}
We consider a downlink cell-free massive MIMO system comprising $M$ single-antenna APs serving $K$ single-antenna users over the same time-frequency resource. The channel coefficient between AP $m$ and user $k$ is denoted by $h_{mk}\in\mathbb{C}$. We adopt a block fading model where the channel is composed of large-scale and small-scale fading components: $h_{mk}=\sqrt{\beta_{mk}} g_{mk}$. Here, $\beta_{mk}$ represents the large-scale fading, encompassing path loss and shadowing, while $g_{mk}\sim \mathcal{CN}(0,1)$ represents the small-scale Rayleigh fading. The collection of all channel coefficients forms the global channel matrix $\mathbf{H}\in \mathbb{C}^{M\times K}$.

The core of our problem is to determine the AP-user associations, represented by a binary matrix $\mathbf{A} \in \{0,1\}^{M \times K}$, where $a_{mk}=1$ if AP $m$ is selected to serve user $k$, and $a_{mk}=0$ otherwise. We impose two structural constraints on $\mathbf{A}$:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{AP Capacity:} Each AP can serve at most one user on a given resource block to avoid intra-AP interference. This translates to the constraint: $\sum_{k=1}^K a_{mk}\le 1$ for all $m \in \{1, \dots, M\}$.
\item \textbf{Per-User Diversity:} Each user must be served by exactly $L$ APs to ensure a minimum quality of service and macro-diversity gain, where $1\le L\le M$. This is expressed as: $\sum_{m=1}^M a_{mk}=L$ for all $k \in \{1, \dots, K\}$.
\end{itemize}

Given a valid selection matrix $\mathbf{A}$, the precoding is designed based on the \emph{effective} channel matrix $\mathbf{H}_A=\mathbf{H}\circ \mathbf{A}$, where $\circ$ denotes the Hadamard (element-wise) product. Let $\mathbf{G}=\mathbf{H}_A^\top\in \mathbb{C}^{K\times M}$. We employ RZF precoding, a powerful linear scheme that balances interference nulling and noise amplification. The unnormalized RZF precoder is given by \cite{peel2005vector}:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{un}}=\mathbf{G}^\mathrm{H}\left(\mathbf{G}\mathbf{G}^\mathrm{H}+\xi \mathbf{I}_K\right)^{-1},
\label{eq:rzf_unnormalized}
\end{equation}
where $\xi>0$ is a regularization parameter, typically proportional to the noise power. To satisfy the per-AP power constraints, we normalize the precoding vector for each AP. Let $\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{un}}(m,:)$ be the $m$-th row of $\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{un}}$. The final precoder $\mathbf{W} \in \mathbb{C}^{M \times K}$ is:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{W}(m,:)=
\begin{cases}
\sqrt{P_m}\,\frac{\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{un}}(m,:)}{\|\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{un}}(m,:)\|_2}, & \text{if } \|\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{un}}(m,:)\|_2 > 0 \\
\mathbf{0}, & \text{otherwise},
\end{cases}
\label{eq:power_norm}
\end{equation}
where $P_m$ is the maximum transmit power of AP $m$.

The signal received at user $k$ is $y_k=\mathbf{h}_k^\mathrm{T}\mathbf{W}\mathbf{s}+n_k$, where $\mathbf{h}_k$ is the $k$-th column of $\mathbf{H}$ (transposed to a row vector), $\mathbf{s}\in\mathbb{C}^{K}$ is the vector of transmit symbols with $\E[\mathbf{s}\mathbf{s}^\mathrm{H}]=\mathbf{I}_K$, and $n_k\sim\mathcal{CN}(0,\sigma_n^2)$ is additive white Gaussian noise. A crucial aspect of our evaluation is that we compute the Signal-to-Interference-plus-Noise Ratio (SINR) using the \emph{full physical channel} $\mathbf{H}$, not the masked channel $\mathbf{H}_A$. This ensures that all sources of interference are correctly accounted for. The SINR for user $k$ is:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{SINR}_k=\frac{|\mathbf{h}_k^\mathrm{T}\mathbf{w}_k|^2}{\sum_{j\neq k}|\mathbf{h}_k^\mathrm{T}\mathbf{w}_j|^2+\sigma_n^2},
\label{eq:sinr}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{w}_j$ is the $j$-th column of $\mathbf{W}$. The achievable rate for user $k$ is $R_k=\log_2(1+\mathrm{SINR}_k)$.

\section{QUBO Formulation for AP–User Selection}
We now detail the formulation of the AP selection problem as a QUBO. The binary variables $a_{mk}$ are flattened into a single vector $\mathbf{x}\in\{0,1\}^{MK}$, where the index $i = (m-1)K + k$ corresponds to the link $(m,k)$. The objective is to find $\mathbf{x}$ that minimizes the quadratic form:
\begin{equation}
E(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}^\top \mathbf{Q}\,\mathbf{x},
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{Q}\in\mathbb{R}^{MK\times MK}$ is a symmetric matrix encoding the problem's objectives and constraints.

\subsection{Utility and Interference Terms}
The diagonal entries of $\mathbf{Q}$ represent the intrinsic utility (or cost) of selecting each link, while the off-diagonal entries encode the cost of selecting pairs of links simultaneously.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Diagonal Terms (Utility):} A good selection should prioritize links with strong channel conditions. We define the utility of link $(m,k)$ as its standalone single-user rate. The corresponding diagonal entry in $\mathbf{Q}$ is the negative of this utility, as QUBO is a minimization problem:
    \begin{equation}
    Q_{ii}=-u_{mk}, \quad \text{where } u_{mk}=\log_2\!\left(1+\frac{P_m|h_{mk}|^2}{\sigma_n^2}\right).
    \end{equation}
    \item \textbf{Off-Diagonal Terms (Interference):} The off-diagonal entries $Q_{ij}$ penalize pairs of links $i=(m,k)$ and $j=(m',k')$ that are likely to cause significant mutual interference. A precise interference model would involve the full RZF precoder, leading to higher-order dependencies that break the quadratic structure of QUBO. As a tractable and effective proxy, we model the pairwise interference cost based on MRT projections. The cost is the reduction in combined rate compared to their standalone rates. Let $r_{mk|m'k'}$ be the rate of link $(m,k)$ when AP $m'$ is transmitting to user $k'$. The penalty is:
    \begin{equation}
    Q_{ij}=c_{ij}=\max\!\Big\{0, (u_{mk}+u_{m'k'}) - (r_{mk|m'k'}+r_{m'k'|mk})\Big\},
    \end{equation}
    for $k \neq k'$. This term captures the fundamental trade-off: selecting two links might provide high individual utilities but come at a high interference cost, which the QUBO formulation explicitly penalizes.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Constraint Penalties}
The "U" in QUBO stands for "unconstrained," meaning hard constraints must be encoded as penalties in the objective function. We use quadratic penalties. For an equality constraint $\sum_{i\in \mathcal{I}} x_i = t$, we add $\lambda \left(\sum_{i\in \mathcal{I}} x_i - t\right)^2$ to the objective. Since $x_i \in \{0,1\}$, $x_i^2 = x_i$. The squared sum expands to:
\begin{equation}
\lambda\left(\sum_{i\in\mathcal{I}}(1-2t)x_i + 2\!\!\sum_{i<j,\,i,j\in\mathcal{I}}\!\! x_ix_j + t^2\right).
\end{equation}
This adds linear terms to the diagonal of $\mathbf{Q}$ and quadratic terms to the off-diagonals. The constant $t^2$ can be ignored.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{AP Capacity:} To enforce $\sum_{k=1}^K x_{(m,k)} \le 1$, we use an equality penalty with target 1. While this is slightly stricter, it is effective in practice for sparse selections.
    \begin{equation}
    E_{\text{AP}}=\lambda_{\text{AP}} \sum_{m=1}^M \bigg(\sum_{k=1}^K x_{(m,k)} - 1\bigg)^2.
    \end{equation}
    \item \textbf{Per-User Diversity:} To enforce $\sum_{m=1}^M x_{(m,k)} = L$:
    \begin{equation}
    E_{\text{U}}=\lambda_{\text{U}} \sum_{k=1}^K \bigg(\sum_{m=1}^M x_{(m,k)} - L\bigg)^2.
    \end{equation}
\end{itemize}
The penalty parameters $\lambda_{\text{AP}}$ and $\lambda_{\text{U}}$ must be chosen large enough to outweigh the utility terms, ensuring that feasible or near-feasible solutions have lower energy.

\subsection{Fairness-Oriented QUBO}
To promote fairness, we can modify the QUBO to prioritize users with poor average channel conditions. We introduce user-specific weights $\omega_k$ into the utility term. A simple and effective weighting scheme is to make $\omega_k$ inversely proportional to the user's average large-scale gain: $\omega_k \propto 1/\left(\frac{1}{M}\sum_m \beta_{mk}\right)$, with the weights normalized such that $\sum_k \omega_k=K$. The diagonal entries of $\mathbf{Q}$ are then modified to $Q_{ii}=-\omega_k u_{mk}$. This encourages the solver to allocate strong links to "cell-edge" users, improving fairness as measured by Jain's index.

\subsection{Mapping to Ising and Solving}
The QUBO is solved using a simulated annealer that mimics the behavior of a CIM. The binary variables $\mathbf{x}\in\{0,1\}^n$ are mapped to Ising spins $\mathbf{s}\in\{-1,1\}^n$ via the transformation $x_i=(1+s_i)/2$. This converts the QUBO into an Ising energy function $H(\mathbf{s})=-\sum_{i<j}J_{ij}s_is_j-\sum_i h_i s_i$, where $J_{ij}$ are couplings and $h_i$ are external fields. The annealer then iteratively flips spins to find low-energy configurations.

\section{Two-Level Algorithm with Feasibility Projection}
The complete pipeline is outlined in Algorithm~\ref{alg:main}. A key step is the feasibility projection. Since annealing is a heuristic, the resulting solution $\mathbf{x}$ may not perfectly satisfy the hard constraints. Instead of discarding this globally-aware solution, we apply a minimal set of deterministic corrections.

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Two-Level QUBO-RZF with Feasibility Projection}
\label{alg:main}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{Input:} Channels $\mathbf{H}$, powers $\{P_m\}$, noise $\sigma_n^2$, service level $L$, penalties $(\lambda_{\text{AP}},\lambda_{\text{U}})$.
\State \textbf{Outer Loop: AP Selection}
\State Build QUBO matrix $\mathbf{Q}$ using utility, interference, and penalty terms.
\State Solve for $\mathbf{x}_{\text{raw}} = \arg\min_{\mathbf{x}} \mathbf{x}^\top\mathbf{Q}\mathbf{x}$ via simulated annealing.
\State Reshape $\mathbf{x}_{\text{raw}}$ to matrix $\mathbf{A}_{\text{raw}}$.
\State \textbf{Feasibility Projection:}
\State Initialize $\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{A}_{\text{raw}}$.
\State For each AP $m$ where $\sum_k a_{mk}>1$, find $k^\star=\arg\max_k |h_{mk}|^2$. Set $a_{mk^\star}=1$ and $a_{mk}=0$ for $k\neq k^\star$.
\State For each user $k$ where $\sum_m a_{mk}<L$, greedily add the ($L-\sum_m a_{mk}$) best available APs (ranked by $|h_{mk}|^2$) to serve user $k$.
\State \textbf{Inner Loop: Precoding and Evaluation}
\State Form effective channel $\mathbf{H}_A=\mathbf{H}\circ \mathbf{A}$.
\State Compute RZF precoder $\mathbf{W}$ from $\mathbf{H}_A$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:rzf_unnormalized}-\ref{eq:power_norm}).
\State Evaluate rates $\{R_k\}$ on the full physical channel $\mathbf{H}$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:sinr}).
\State Compute sum rate $\sum_k R_k$ and Jain's fairness index.
\State \textbf{Output:} Selection $\mathbf{A}$, precoder $\mathbf{W}$, performance metrics.
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Experimental Setup}

\subsection{Baselines for Comparison}
To evaluate our QUBO-based framework, we compare it against four relevant baselines. For a fair comparison, each baseline's selection matrix $\mathbf{A}$ is passed to the same inner RZF precoding and rate evaluation stage (Algorithm \ref{alg:main}, lines 10-13).
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Random Selection}: For each user, $L$ distinct APs are chosen uniformly at random from the set of available APs. This serves as a lower bound on performance.
\item \textbf{Max-Channel-Gain (Max-CG)}: A common user-centric heuristic. Each user independently selects the $L$ APs that offer the highest instantaneous channel gain $|h_{mk}|^2$. Conflicts (where an AP is chosen by multiple users) are resolved greedily.
\item \textbf{Greedy Sum-Rate (Greedy-SR)}: An iterative, myopic algorithm. Starting with an empty selection, it repeatedly adds the single feasible link $(m,k)$ that provides the largest marginal increase to the total network sum rate, until each user is served by $L$ APs.
\item \textbf{Greedy Max-Min (Greedy-MM)}: Similar to Greedy-SR, but the objective is to maximize the minimum user rate at each step. This is a common heuristic for achieving max-min fairness.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Simulation Parameters}
We simulate a square area of $1\times 1$ km$^2$ where $M$ APs and $K$ users are placed uniformly at random in each realization. We use a standard urban macrocell path loss model from 3GPP \cite{bjornson2016massive} with log-normal shadowing. Small-scale fading is Rayleigh. All performance metrics are averaged over 50 independent Monte Carlo simulations (seeds), each with a new device placement and small-scale fading realization. We report means and 95\% confidence intervals. Key parameters are summarized in Table \ref{tab:params}.

