\section{Complexity Analysis}
The computational complexity of our framework can be broken down into three stages:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{QUBO Construction:} The dominant cost is computing the $O((MK)^2)$ off-diagonal interference terms. This gives a complexity of $O(M^2K^2)$.
    \item \textbf{QUBO Solving:} The simulated annealer performs $T$ temperature steps. In each step, it attempts $O(MK)$ spin flips. Each flip requires calculating the energy delta, which takes $O(MK)$ time. The total complexity is thus $O(T \cdot (MK)^2)$. While high, this stage is the target for hardware acceleration by physical Ising machines.
    \item \textbf{Precoding and Evaluation:} The RZF precoder requires a $K \times K$ matrix inversion, costing $O(K^3)$, plus $O(MK)$ for normalization. Rate evaluation for all users costs $O(MK^2)$.
\end{enumerate}
Overall, building and solving the QUBO is the bottleneck for a classical simulation. The greedy baselines are also computationally intensive, as they require re-evaluating rates for many candidate links at each of the $KL$ selection steps.

