We present our main findings in Figure~\ref{fig:main_results}, which shows the sum spectral efficiency, Jain's fairness index, and the trade-off between these two metrics. We evaluate five methods: the two proposed QUBO variants (sum-rate oriented QUBO-SR, and fairness-oriented QUBO-Fair) and three baselines (Max-CG, Greedy-SR, and Greedy-MM).

\subsection{Sum Spectral Efficiency}
Figure~\ref{fig:main_results}(a) shows the sum spectral efficiency as a function of the number of users, $K$. As expected, the sum rate increases with $K$ for all methods due to increased multiuser diversity, until the system becomes interference-limited. The QUBO-SR method consistently outperforms all baselines. Notably, it achieves a statistically significant gain over the strong Greedy-SR baseline for $K \ge 6$, as indicated by the non-overlapping 95\% confidence intervals. This highlights the key advantage of the QUBO's global perspective: by considering all pairwise interference costs simultaneously, it finds AP-user pairings that are more spatially compatible than those found by myopic, greedy decisions. The QUBO-Fair method incurs a slight, predictable penalty in sum-rate as it prioritizes fairness.

\subsection{Jain's Fairness Index}
Figure~\ref{fig:main_results}(b) illustrates the network fairness. The QUBO-Fair method achieves the highest Jain's fairness index across all user loads, significantly outperforming all other methods. This demonstrates the effectiveness of the large-scale-fading-based weighting scheme in protecting vulnerable users. Interestingly, even the standard QUBO-SR method provides better fairness than Greedy-SR, suggesting that its superior interference management inherently leads to more balanced performance. The Greedy-MM baseline, designed for fairness, improves upon Greedy-SR but is still surpassed by QUBO-Fair, which achieves a better balance.

\subsection{Performance Trade-off}
The trade-off between sum rate and fairness is explicitly shown in Figure~\ref{fig:main_results}(c) for a system with $K=8$ users. Each point represents the average performance of a method. The ideal operating point is in the top-right corner. The plot clearly shows that the QUBO-based methods occupy a superior region of the trade-off space. QUBO-SR provides the highest sum rate, while QUBO-Fair achieves a large gain in fairness for a small reduction in sum rate. This demonstrates the flexibility of the proposed framework to cater to different quality-of-service requirements by simply adjusting the weights in the QUBO objective function.

\section{Discussion and Limitations}
Our results demonstrate the promise of a QUBO-based approach for resource allocation in cell-free networks. However, several practical challenges and limitations must be acknowledged.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{CSI Acquisition and Fronthaul:} Our centralized framework assumes that the central processing unit has access to global CSI for all $MK$ links. In practice, acquiring this CSI incurs significant pilot overhead, and communicating it over fronthaul links consumes bandwidth. These overheads, which are not modeled in this work, are critical constraints for real-world deployment \cite{demir2021foundations}. Future work could explore decentralized or hierarchical QUBO formulations to mitigate this burden. The impact of imperfect CSI due to estimation errors and feedback delay is also an important area for investigation, as channel hardening is less pronounced in cell-free systems compared to co-located massive MIMO \cite{chen2018channel}.
    \item \textbf{QUBO Fidelity and Scalability:} The pairwise interference terms in our QUBO are an approximation of the true multiuser interference under RZF precoding. While empirically effective, this model may not capture all higher-order effects. The primary limitation, however, is scalability. The number of variables in the QUBO is $MK$, and the number of couplings in our dense formulation is $O(M^2K^2)$. While this is manageable for the system sizes considered, scaling to hundreds of APs would require problem decomposition or sparsification techniques.
    \item \textbf{Hardware Realizability:} The practical advantage of our approach hinges on the availability and performance of physical Ising solvers. Current-generation CIMs and quantum annealers have limitations on the number of spins (variables) and the density of the coupling graph they can support. Mapping our dense QUBO problem onto hardware with restricted connectivity may require embedding techniques, which can introduce additional overhead.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}
We have introduced a novel two-level optimization framework for joint AP selection and precoding in cell-free massive MIMO systems. By decomposing the problem into a discrete selection task solved via a QUBO and a continuous precoding task solved with RZF, our approach effectively navigates the complex combinatorial search space. The proposed QUBO formulation, which incorporates principled utility and interference terms, demonstrates statistically significant performance gains in sum rate over strong greedy baselines. Furthermore, a fairness-oriented variant of the QUBO achieves substantial improvements in user fairness with only a minor impact on overall throughput. Our work highlights the potential of physics-inspired optimization as a powerful tool for complex resource allocation problems in future wireless networks and provides a flexible framework compatible with emerging hardware accelerators. Future research will focus on robust formulations that account for imperfect CSI and hierarchical QUBO decompositions to enhance scalability for very large-scale deployments.

